should you pay your first employees  salary or equity this is a great  question that came in from one of my  YouTube viewers PAC media who it in  which term should I hire my first few  employees salary or equity almost always  I would pick salary if you had a choice  between the two the reason is you  usually make mistakes on your first  hires if you look at most successful  business and you look at who their first  hire was they're rarely still with the  business so you don't want somebody  who's not with the business long term to  have equity in your company you have to  be really careful about who you give it  equity to and how much when you first  start hiring people it's exciting right  you have somebody on your team you're  you're giving away the tax that you  don't want to do you're hiring someone  who is better than you at some of these  tasks and it lets you focus on the  bigger picture stuff to grow your  business at and it's great and I highly  recommend it the challenge is if you've  never hired people before you're going  to make mistakes right you're going to  make mistakes in managing you're going  to make the mistakes in interviewing  you're going to make mistakes and  actually bring them on board and in  training your company will probably  change its product or service a number  of times and maybe the skills that you  hired that first person for are not  useful anymore you're going to make  mistakes your business is going to  change so the people you bring on board  may not be the right fit for you long  term with the business so I would  generally lean towards salary now you  may not have a lot of salary that's the  thing a little great Evan let's give  them all Sally but I don't have any  money to pay these guys there's a couple  ways you can do it okay  first hire part-time the first guy I  hired was for one hour a day one hour a  day to free up my time to do other  things  I hate hiring people I hate spending  money until I'm making money so I at the  start of any project put in my own my  own guts my own my own effort my own  energy I'm doing everything even if I'm  not great at it  I start doing it myself to learn about  it see if I want to keep doing this if  I'm excited by it to start trying to  make some money at it and if I make some  money then I start investing that back  into the project and that might be  technology it might be resources but it  might be people  right I might delegate this off to  somebody else to do but I know I'm  committed to it this is the path I'm  going to follow I'm not just testing a  few things and I know I have a way to  make money so I know it can afford to  pay this person so I encourage  entrepreneurs at the start to do  everything yourself and once you start  making money and you know you're going  to continue on this path bring on people  to support you but it doesn't have to be  a full-time person start part-time and  build up from there another way to do it  is based on success of projects and this  is where you have a little more trust  with the person if they have something  that can be measured can be defined  easily then you can give them a split of  the revenue comes in from that project  you're not giving up equity in your  business but you're giving them  ownership of a specific project that if  it makes money they're going to make  part of it back as well now the  exception if you find somebody who you  think could really be a good long-term  piece of your business  there could be an option to give them  some equity I want to warn you though  bringing on a partner is like getting  married you have to really know them  like them trust them believe they have  the same values as you have the same  goals and vision as you it's not just  somebody off the street it's really  really hard to find good partners and if  you bring on the wrong person it could  sink your business they could sink your  business even a minority shareholder can  hold a lot of stuff back from your  business are moving forward and you  don't need people who are not in line  with what you're trying to do with the  business you need something to  contribute be there long term believing  the same thing you do have the same  values and be on board with the plan for  the business if not you can't give up  equity if you are going to give up some  equity I would strongly also encourage  you to have some kind of build in  program so they'll get it right from the  start they have to prove themselves they  have some milestones to reach both in  terms of how long they're with you for  and in terms of results that you're  looking for in one of my software  companies I started with a business  partner mine we had another business  together so I work well with him and we  were bringing on a third person to be  our salesperson and he worked out great  at the start and we looked at giving him  some equity  it wasn't just oh here here's you know  30% of the business  go forth he had to earn in he had to go  out and meet quotas he had to go and  sell he had to meet these expectations  and after three months he got a certain  amount six months a certain amount a  year a certain amount of equity if he  hit his milestones and it's laid out  from the beginning so those expectations  of what to do but more important than  just reaching the results is again  making sure they had the same values as  you do and they're right fit long term  from your business you see this as  somebody who you can work with even if  it wasn't this business something  totally different you want to work with  this person long-term in tons of  businesses that's when you want to bring  on an equity partner so for the most  part I'm looking salary I'm looking  part-time at the beginning and I'm  looking to only spend when I'm making a  little bit of money building that up to  eventually getting full-time people and  building up to going from one person to  two people to five people to ten people  to as many as you need to grow this  thing and in the rare circumstance that  you find somebody who would be a good  partner for your business let them work  with them over a period of time to make  sure you guys agree on things and have a  system where they have to earn in or buy  in over a period of time when they are  meeting their deadlines there's no hard  and fast rule each person each case you  got to take one by one that's part of  the thrill and the challenge of being an  entrepreneur but if you do it properly  it can make all the difference in the  world for your business believe for  those you're watching like the video  please give it a thumbs up below it  makes you want to do more for you and if  you have a comment or a question leave  it below  I love seem to come in they make my day  every day so I really appreciate it  those comments keep me going and doing  what I'm doing so thank you so much  leave a comment and I'll see you soon  you
 

